  "inline_comments": [
    {
      "body": "I also think DataProtoItem is not a good design and should be removed. However, when item is a single index, the batch dim vanishes. Is there any way to handle both cases?",
      "created_at": "2025-03-12T03:21:25Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -195,6 +195,15 @@ def __len__(self):\n             return 0\n \n     def __getitem__(self, item):\n+        if isinstance(item, slice):\n+            if self.batch is not None:\n+                tensor_data = self.batch[item]\n+            else:\n+                tensor_data = None\n+\n+            non_tensor_data = {key: val[item] for key, val in self.non_tensor_batch.items()}\n+            return DataProto(batch=tensor_data, non_tensor_batch=non_tensor_data, meta_info=self.meta_info)\n+\n         tensor_data = self.batch[item]\n         non_tensor_data = {key: val[item] for key, val in self.non_tensor_batch.items()}\n         return DataProtoItem(batch=tensor_data, non_tensor_batch=non_tensor_data, meta_info=self.meta_info)",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pull/547#discussion_r1990483189",
      "id": 1990483189,
      "line": 209,
      "original_line": 209,
      "path": "verl/protocol.py",
      "updated_at": "2025-03-12T03:21:45Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "vermouth1992"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "We could always maintain the dimensionality for both batch and non_tensor_batch, and then add a squeeze function to DataProto. However, int indices removing a dimension while slices maintain it is fairly common in Python (lists, numpy, torch, etc.) so it's not necessarily a bad thing.\r\n\r\nI am far too new to the code base to know what the appropriate call is though",
      "created_at": "2025-03-12T14:38:40Z",
      "diff_hunk": "@@ -195,6 +195,15 @@ def __len__(self):\n             return 0\n \n     def __getitem__(self, item):\n+        if isinstance(item, slice):\n+            if self.batch is not None:\n+                tensor_data = self.batch[item]\n+            else:\n+                tensor_data = None\n+\n+            non_tensor_data = {key: val[item] for key, val in self.non_tensor_batch.items()}\n+            return DataProto(batch=tensor_data, non_tensor_batch=non_tensor_data, meta_info=self.meta_info)\n+\n         tensor_data = self.batch[item]\n         non_tensor_data = {key: val[item] for key, val in self.non_tensor_batch.items()}\n         return DataProtoItem(batch=tensor_data, non_tensor_batch=non_tensor_data, meta_info=self.meta_info)",
